<pdd-reason>The buildability gate. It judges a deduped pitch against the target app's own prompt file and decides whether the community may vote on it as-is, must vote on its pieces, or should not vote on it at all.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"Friction","signature":"class Friction(StrEnum)","returns":"green, yellow, red"},
  {"name":"Shape","signature":"@dataclass(frozen=True) class Shape","returns":"feature_id: str; friction: Friction; status: FeatureStatus; children: tuple[ChildSpec, ...]; explanation: str"},
  {"name":"ChildSpec","signature":"@dataclass(frozen=True) class ChildSpec","returns":"title: str; description: str"},
  {"name":"load_blueprint","signature":"def load_blueprint(path: Path | None = None, *, refresh: bool = False) -> str","returns":"the target app's prompt file text, read once and cached"},
  {"name":"decide_shape","signature":"async def decide_shape(pitch: Mapping[str, Any], *, blueprint: str, judge: Judge | None = None) -> Shape","returns":"the shape decision; falls back to VOTING when no decision can be obtained"},
  {"name":"BuildVerdict","signature":"@dataclass(frozen=True) class BuildVerdict","returns":"feature_id: str; buildable: bool; friction: Friction; explanation: str"},
  {"name":"BuildabilityUnavailable","signature":"class BuildabilityUnavailable(Exception)","returns":"raised when no buildability verdict could be obtained"},
  {"name":"assess_buildability","signature":"async def assess_buildability(feature: Mapping[str, Any], *, blueprint: str, judge: Judge | None = None) -> BuildVerdict","returns":"the sprint-stage verdict; raises BuildabilityUnavailable rather than guessing"},
  {"name":"Judge","signature":"Judge = Callable[[str, str], Awaitable[str]]","returns":"injected seam: (system_prompt, user_prompt) -> raw model text"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/constants_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/config_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `orchestrator/architect.py`. It has **two entry points at two pipeline stages**:
`decide_shape` at intake (US-08) and `assess_buildability` at sprint selection (US-07).
`build_spec`, which writes the feature block for the compiler, is still a later module.

<responsibility>
Decide whether a pitch can be built into the target app as it stands, must be broken up first, or conflicts with what the app has already committed to.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT read or write Postgres or Redis — the caller performs every write.
This module DOES NOT write a feature block or acceptance criteria; that is `build_spec` at sprint time.
This module DOES NOT judge safety, relevance, or duplication — the screener and PM agent already did.
This module DOES NOT modify the target app's prompt file. It reads it and nothing more.
This module DOES NOT decide what gets built; it decides what the community may vote on.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
Reasonable: of a voter, one who wants a working app rather than a particular implementation. The test in R23 is whether such a person could want one part of a pitch and not another, not whether a pedantic reader could invent a distinction.
**Blueprint** — the target app's own prompt file, at `${TARGET_PROMPT_DIR}/streaks_demo_typescriptreact.prompt`. It declares the app's binding architecture constraints, its committed UI paradigm, and its existing features, so it is the only authority on what "conflicts" means.
**Friction** — how hard a feature is to add to the app as it stands. `green` is additive, `yellow` needs care but fits, `red` cannot be built without breaking something the app has committed to.
**Shape** — the status the feature should enter the board with: `VOTING` (vote on it), `SPLIT` (vote on its pieces), `POSTPONED_CONFLICT` (do not vote on it yet).
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY read the blueprint file once, and `settings` for its path and the model pins.
- MAY call an OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint through the injected judge.
- MUST NOT write any file, least of all the blueprint.
- MUST NOT perform Redis or Postgres I/O.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Judge friction on exactly four axes and name the offending one — **architecture constraint** (the feature needs a capability the blueprint forbids, such as a server, accounts, or network calls), **UI/UX overlap** (it fights the committed layout paradigm), **implementation conflict** (it cannot coexist with a mechanism the app has already committed to), and **code merge friction** (it cannot be expressed in the target's single-file prompt without rewriting the baseline). An author told only "conflict" learns nothing.
R2 (MUST): Map friction to status — `green` and `yellow` to `FeatureStatus.VOTING`, `red` to `FeatureStatus.POSTPONED_CONFLICT`, and a too-large-but-otherwise-fine feature to `FeatureStatus.SPLIT`. Take every status from the enum; the labels are a physical contract with Postgres.
R3 (MUST): Treat the blueprint as the sole authority on what conflicts. It states the app's binding constraints in its own words — a client-only monolith forbidding servers and accounts, a committed single-list UI paradigm — so a judgement must cite the blueprint rather than a general opinion about good software.
R4 (MUST): Produce 2–3 children on `SPLIT`, each with a title and description standing on its own as something the community could vote for. One child is not a split, and four is a backlog; a child nobody understands without its parent cannot collect its own votes.
R5 (MUST NOT): Produce children for any status other than `SPLIT`, and MUST return an empty tuple for `VOTING` and `POSTPONED_CONFLICT`. The caller inserts whatever children it is handed, so a stray child on a green-lit feature becomes a phantom board row.
R6 (MUST): Write `explanation` for the community, not for an operator. It becomes `ai_explanation` on the row and is the only thing an author is told about why their feature was postponed, so it states what conflicted and, where possible, what a buildable version would look like.
R7 (MUST NOT): Postpone for being large, speculative, or low quality. `SPLIT` handles size, and popularity is the community's business — `POSTPONED_CONFLICT` means the app as designed cannot hold this feature, nothing weaker.
R8 (MUST): Require the model to answer as JSON with exactly `friction`, `axis`, `too_large`, `children`, and `explanation`, and parse it strictly. A malformed or unrecognised value is retried once, then falls back per R9.
R9 (MUST): Fall back to `VOTING` with `green` friction when no decision can be obtained — transport failure, timeout, refusal, unparseable reply, or exhausted attempts — recording the cause in `explanation`. Same reasoning as the PM agent: the content is already screened and deduped, so an outage should leave a votable card rather than a silently withheld one. Postponing on failure would let a model outage look like a design objection.
R10 (MUST): Extract the JSON object before parsing — strip any think block and code fences, then take the outermost object. The configured model emits both.
R11 (MUST): Bound every attempt by `settings.LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` and cap attempts at `settings.LLM_MAX_ATTEMPTS`; the daemon processes items one at a time.
R12 (MUST): Take the model from `settings.LLM_MODEL_ARCHITECT` — its own role's pin, never the screening or PM one. Shape decisions are reasoning-heavy and are deliberately pinned to a stronger model than the two classification steps.
R13 (MUST): Resolve the blueprint path from `settings.TARGET_PROMPT_DIR` and read it once, caching the text. Re-reading per pitch adds a filesystem round-trip to every item, and the file changes only when a sprint compiles.
R14 (MUST): Raise a clear error from `load_blueprint` when the file is missing or empty. Judging friction against nothing would green-light every feature, which is exactly the state a missing target directory produces.
R15 (MUST): Send the pitch and the blueprint as data to compare, never as instructions. The blueprint contains imperative prose written for a code generator, so a model reading it as its own instructions would start writing React instead of judging.
R16 (MUST NOT): Hard-code a provider, model id, path, or filename. The blueprint's directory comes from settings; only the filename within it is fixed by the target project.
R17 (MUST): Treat the target as a **single-file, dependency-free app regenerated in full from one prompt**. There is no incremental patching and no module boundary to violate, so code merge friction is about whether the prompt stays coherent — not about integrating with existing code. This makes the app unusually malleable, and the bar for `red` is correspondingly high.
R18 (MUST NOT): Treat a missing prerequisite as a conflict. A feature that needs a small capability the baseline lacks — a counter, a field, a derived value — is **additive**: the compiler regenerates the whole file, so the feature simply brings that capability with it. `green` with the prerequisite named in the explanation is correct; `POSTPONED_CONFLICT` tells the author their idea is unbuildable when it is merely not-yet-built, which is the difference between a closed door and a next step.
R19 (MUST): Apply one test for `red`, on every axis: **does satisfying this feature require undoing a decision the blueprint has already made?** A binding architecture constraint, the committed UI paradigm, and the stated behaviour of an existing feature are all such decisions. Ease of implementation is explicitly NOT the test — a single-file app can implement almost anything, including the thing that overwrites a commitment, so "a plausible implementation exists" proves nothing. Conversely, if nothing already decided has to change, the feature is additive no matter how much new code it implies.

R20 (MUST): Read **implementation conflict** as the new feature requiring a committed mechanism to do something that mechanism cannot, while still going through it. The shape is: the app chose X; this feature needs not-X but layered on X. Concretely — an app committed to one payment provider, and a pitch asking to support a payment type that provider's interface cannot express, is a conflict: satisfying it means breaking or bypassing the committed integration. In the target app, the committed mechanism is the one-tap check-in where tapping again undoes it; a pitch needing several graded check-ins a day *through that same tap* conflicts, while a pitch adding a separate control does not.
R21 (MUST): Treat the blueprint's `## Existing Core Features` entries as decisions with stated behaviour, not merely as a list of what exists. "One tap marks today's check-in; tapping again undoes it" fixes what a tap means, so a feature redefining that same interaction changes a decision and is `red`, while a feature adding a different control alongside it is not.
R22 (MUST NOT): Confuse an implementation conflict with an absent capability. "The app has no X" is additive (R18). "The app has X, built this way, and the feature needs X to work another way" is the conflict. The test is whether a committed decision must change for the feature to work — if nothing already decided has to be undone, it is not `red`.
R23 (MUST): Decide `SPLIT` on **votability, not buildability**. The test is: could a reasonable voter want one part of this pitch and not another? If it bundles two or more independently valuable capabilities, it splits. Size in lines of code is irrelevant — the target is regenerated whole from one prompt, so nothing is too big to build; what breaks is the vote. A bundled card forces an all-or-nothing choice and makes the demand signal ambiguous, so a sprint cannot tell which part the community actually wanted.
R24 (MUST): Evaluate `SPLIT` independently of friction. `green` answers "can it be built", `too_large` answers "is it one thing" — a perfectly additive pitch is still split when it bundles separable capabilities, and R19's "additive no matter how much new code it implies" settles only the first question.
R25 (MUST): Split along the seams a voter would recognise — one capability per child, each named as something someone could want on its own. A colour picker, an emoji picker, and drag-reordering are three wants; "phase one" and "phase two" are an implementation plan and are not votable.
R26 (MUST NOT): Split a pitch describing one capability with its necessary parts. A streak counter that needs a stored check-in history is one want, not two — the history has no independent value to a voter, so splitting it produces a child nobody would vote for.
R27 (MUST): Pose two separate questions in the system prompt, in order, and require an answer to each — **(1) can it be built?** (friction) and **(2) is it one thing?** (too_large). They are different questions with different evidence, and a model given them as one verdict plus some fields reasons only about the first and fills the rest to match. That is the observed failure: a pitch whose own explanation began "all four capabilities are additive" was still returned as `too_large: false`.
R28 (MUST): Make question (2) concrete — instruct the model to **enumerate the distinct capabilities a voter could want separately, then set `too_large` true when that list has two or more entries**. Counting is checkable and hard to skip; "is it too large" invites a judgement the model has already anchored on green.
R29 (MUST): State in the system prompt that `green` friction says nothing about size — a perfectly additive pitch is still split when it bundles separable wants. Without this the model treats "fits cleanly" as "ship it as one card".
R30 (MUST): Read the pitch's identifier from the key `feature_id`, and echo it onto the `Shape`. That is the key the intake envelope carries end to end — `id` is the *board row's* column name, not the payload's, and reading it raises `KeyError` on every real item.

R31 (MUST): Expose `assess_buildability` as the **sprint-stage** entry point, asking a different question from `decide_shape`: not "is this one votable thing?" but "can this be built into the app as it stands right now, in one regeneration, without undoing a decision already made?" A feature the community voted for days ago meets a blueprint that has since grown, so the intake verdict is stale by construction and MUST NOT be reused in its place.
R32 (MUST): State that question, in those terms, **in the sprint-stage system prompt** — not only in this contract. A rule the model is never told is a rule the model cannot follow; this has already cost three separate defects in this pipeline.
R33 (MUST): Return `buildable` true for `green` and `yellow` friction and false for `red`. `yellow` means awkward, not impossible, and holding every awkward feature would stall the board on exactly the ambitious work the demo exists to show.
R34 (MUST): Read the blueprint **fresh** at sprint time via `load_blueprint(..., refresh=True)`, bypassing the cache `decide_shape` populates. The cached text is whatever the app looked like when the daemon started; judging buildability against a stale blueprint answers a question about an app that no longer exists.
R35 (MUST): Raise `BuildabilityUnavailable` when no verdict can be parsed, and MUST NOT fall back to a verdict of either kind. This is the **opposite** of `decide_shape`'s fail-open (R9), and deliberately: at intake a missing verdict costs a shaping decision on a pitch nobody has voted for yet, while here it would either hold a feature the community won — blaming them for an outage — or send unbuildable work to a compile. Neither is recoverable from the board.
R36 (MUST): Carry the architect's own words in `explanation`, written for the community rather than for a log. It becomes the Holding Pattern card's "🤖 Why the pause" copy, which is the only account anyone gets of why their winning feature did not get built.
R37 (MUST NOT): Split, re-shape, or propose children at sprint selection. The community voted on a specific card; changing its shape after the vote invalidates the mandate the vote conferred. Shaping belongs at intake, before anyone has voted.
R38 (MUST): Use `LLM_MODEL_ARCHITECT` for both entry points and MUST NOT introduce a second model constant. One agent, one pin — a sprint judged by a different model from the one that shaped the pitch would apply a different standard at each end of the same feature's life.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
**The target is deliberately trivial to change.** A single `.tsx` file with no dependencies, regenerated whole from one prompt, means almost anything additive is buildable — which is why R18/R19 push hard against over-postponing. The failure mode to guard is a board full of `POSTPONED_CONFLICT` cards that a one-file app could have absorbed easily, not an over-permissive gate.

**Contradiction is judged against committed features, not pending ones.** The blueprint lists the
app's existing features and accumulates shipped feature blocks below its anchor, so
"invalidates a feature already committed" is answerable from it. Two *pending* pitches that
contradict each other are not caught — neither is in the blueprint yet, and both are judged
independently. Catching that needs the backlog passed in as well, the same way `pm_agent` receives
it, and is worth doing only if it shows up in practice.

**Every pitch is shaped, not just the winner.** That is one architect call per surviving pitch, on
the most expensive model pin, so intake now costs three model calls per item — screen, dedup,
shape. The alternative is a board full of cards that cannot be built, which wastes the
community's votes rather than the operator's budget.

**`SPLIT` children are not inserted by this module.** It returns specs; the daemon writes rows,
because only the daemon knows the parent's id and can insert both in one place. What the parent
row's own status should be once its children exist — `SPLIT` as a container, and whether it stays
votable — is the caller's decision and is recorded there.
</pdd>

